{{Japanese name|Taku}}

'''[[Mitsunari Taku]]''' ({{nihongo|卓 光成|Taku Mitsunari}}; 21 January 1835 – 7 September 1924) was a Sanese politician who served as the 4th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for two non-consecutive terms: first from 1889 to 1890, and again from 1893 to 1896. A conservative elder statesman ({{nihongo|genrō|元老}}) who strongly opposed the formation of political parties, Taku's first term oversaw the transition to the first Diet elections, while his second term guided the nation through the [[Lurandian Expansion]] and the signing of the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]].

==Early life and education==
[[Mitsunari Taku]] was born in [[Kofu Prefecture|Kofu]] in 1835, the son of a provincial samurai family of modest means. Unlike many of the genrō who came from the great domains of Mito or Satsuma, Taku rose through the ranks of the Shogunal bureaucracy on the strength of his intellectual abilities rather than his lineage.

Taku studied at the Shogunal academy in Kofu before being sent to [[Okami Imperial University]] after the [[Saito Restoration]], where he specialized in international law and diplomacy. His expertise in foreign affairs would define his career, and he became one of the few Sanese leaders of his generation who could negotiate with [[Dagit]] and [[Illypnian]] diplomats on equal terms.

==Political career==
Taku entered the Saito government in 1873 as a senior official in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. He was appointed [[Minister of Foreign Affairs (Okaiken)|Minister of Foreign Affairs]] in 1886, a position he held until 1889. In this role, he continued the difficult negotiations over the revision of the unequal treaties imposed by [[Dagit]], though like his predecessors he was unable to secure meaningful concessions.

As a member of the [[Genrō Council]], Taku was a staunch opponent of the emerging political party movement. He believed that parties would introduce factionalism and corruption into the clean, efficient governance that the Saito Restoration had sought to establish. This position put him at odds with reformers like [[Ryuichi Koyanagi]], who saw parties as essential to democratic accountability.

==First Premiership (1889–1890)==
[[File:First Diet Elections 1890.png|thumb|right|200px|The first Diet elections of 1890, which Taku managed as interim prime minister]]
Taku was appointed Prime Minister on 5 September 1889, succeeding [[Kanezane Enoki]] on the very day the [[Saito Constitution]] came into effect. His primary task was to manage the transition to the first Diet elections, scheduled for 1890.

The Taku government established the electoral districts, appointed the prefectural governors who would oversee the voting, and drafted the regulations governing the Diet's procedures. However, Taku's deep suspicion of political parties led him to attempt to manage the elections in a way that would minimize the influence of party politicians. He instructed prefectural governors to discourage party organizing and to favor "independent" candidates who would support the government.

This strategy backfired. The elections produced a Diet in which party-aligned politicians held a significant bloc of seats, and the government found itself unable to command a reliable majority. Taku attempted to form a government that could work with the Diet, but his refusal to compromise with party leaders made this impossible.

On the civil service front, Taku introduced the Civil Service Examination Reform of 1890, which standardized the examination process for government appointments and established merit-based promotion criteria. While intended to professionalize the bureaucracy, the reform also served Taku's goal of excluding party politicians from administrative positions, as the examination system favored candidates with classical education over those with practical political experience.

He resigned on 12 December 1890, after only fifteen months in office, having failed to establish a working relationship with the newly elected legislature.

==Second Premiership (1893–1896)==
Taku returned to power on 10 January 1893, following the resignation of [[Juichi Miyazaki]]'s second government. The intervening period had seen the first experiment in party government collapse and the political system descend into factional chaos. The genrō council turned to Taku as a "transcendent" prime minister—one who would govern above party politics.

The defining challenge of Taku's second term was the [[Lurandian Expansion]], the period of intensified colonial competition among the [[Illypnian]] powers for control of the [[Shendan Ocean]] and its trade routes. As Illypnian navies projected power into the Shendan, Okaiken faced the strategic imperative of securing its own maritime interests and protecting the Sanese diaspora in Lurandia.

Taku's most significant achievement was the negotiation and signing of the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] in 1895.
[[File:Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty Signing 1895.png|thumb|right|200px|The signing of the Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding in 1895]]
This landmark agreement established a framework of mutual recognition and non-aggression between the Empire of Mitoyagi and the Zong Empire, and included provisions for commercial cooperation and cultural exchange. The treaty was widely seen as a diplomatic masterstroke that secured Okaiken's continental flank and allowed the nation to focus its strategic attention on the Shendan.

The Taku government also oversaw the rapid modernization of the Imperial Sanese Navy, which became the dominant naval power in the [[Shendan Ocean]]. Naval construction programs initiated during this period laid the foundation for the fleet that would project Sanese power throughout the region for the next half-century.

Despite these foreign policy successes, Taku's domestic position remained precarious. He continued to resist the formation of political parties, and his government relied on the support of the genrō council and the Imperial Household rather than on any parliamentary majority. When the Diet refused to pass his budget in 1896, Taku dissolved it and governed by imperial decree—a controversial move that further eroded the legitimacy of the "transcendent" approach.

The Taku government also achieved the long-sought revision of the [[Dagit]] unequal treaties. Building on the diplomatic groundwork laid by Enoki and Konoe, Taku's negotiators secured the Treaty of Commerce and Navigation of 1894, which restored tariff autonomy and gradually eliminated extraterritoriality. The treaty revision was widely celebrated as a diplomatic triumph—the end of the "Dagit humiliation" that had plagued Sanese foreign policy for two decades—and it significantly enhanced the government's domestic standing.

In the Shansian territorial disputes, Taku pursued a firm but diplomatic approach. He reinforced Sanese claims to the northern islands through diplomatic notes and limited naval deployments, while avoiding the provocative military actions that the Army General Staff advocated. The Shansian government, preoccupied with its own internal challenges, did not escalate the dispute, and a fragile status quo was maintained.

Taku resigned on 2 October 1896, citing exhaustion and declining health. He was succeeded by [[Juichi Miyazaki]], who returned for a third term.

==Legacy==
Mitsunari Taku is remembered as the last of the great "transcendent" prime ministers who attempted to govern without political parties. His failure to establish a working relationship with the Diet demonstrated the unsustainability of the genrō-dominated system, and his resignation paved the way for the first party-based government of [[Ryuichi Koyanagi]] in 1898.

At the same time, Taku's foreign policy achievements—particularly the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] and the naval modernization program—had far-reaching consequences. The treaty with Zong reshaped the strategic landscape of the Shendan region, and the navy he built would become the instrument of Sanese power projection for decades to come.

After his resignation, Taku retired from active politics but continued to serve as a genrō, advising subsequent governments on foreign policy. He died in [[Okami]] on 7 September 1924 at the age of 89, the longest-lived of the Saito-era genrō.

